<macros>
    <token name="@TOOL_VERSION@">0.3.1.2</token>
    <token name="@REQUIREMENT_VERSION@">0.3.1</token>
    <!-- STOPGAP: the two extra requirements pin the Bioconductor stack.
         dante_tir's conda recipe pins no Bioconductor versions at all
         (bioconductor-biostrings / -bsgenome / -rsamtools / -rtracklayer are
         unpinned and biocgenerics only arrives transitively), so one package
         version can resolve to different stacks on different machines. On the
         production Galaxy, detect_tirs.R died at load time with

             package or namespace load failed for 'BiocGenerics' ...
             undefined exports: start<-, end<-, width, ...

         which is a NAMESPACE/code mismatch, not a missing package. The pins below
         are the combination verified to work: the solve is conflict-free, it
         resolves the r43 / Bioconductor 3.18 stack (BiocGenerics 0.48.1,
         S4Vectors 0.40.2, IRanges 2.36.0, Biostrings 2.70.1), BiocGenerics loads,
         and dante_tir runs the committed fixture to its expected 4 TIRs.

         Note this also changes the environment Galaxy builds: with more than one
         requirement, resolve_all names it mulled-v1-<hash> instead of
         __dante_tir@0.3.1, so a fresh env is created rather than the existing one
         reused. See kavonrtep/dante_tir - remove these two lines once the recipe
         pins its own Bioconductor stack, since duplicating a recipe's pins here
         will otherwise drift. -->
    <xml name="requirements">
        <requirements>
            <requirement type="package" version="@REQUIREMENT_VERSION@">dante_tir</requirement>
            <requirement type="package" version="4.3.3">r-base</requirement>
            <requirement type="package" version="0.48.1">bioconductor-biocgenerics</requirement>
        </requirements>
    </xml>
    <!-- Error detection. Replaces detect_errors="aggressive", which fails a job on any
         stdout/stderr line matching "error:" (case-insensitive, fatal). mmseqs2 prints
         "there must be an error: N deleted from M that now is empty, but not assigned to
         a cluster" as ordinary clustering chatter - the string is compiled into the
         binary - and that alone marked a completed TideCluster run as failed: exit status
         0, every output written in full. Any wrapper whose program reaches mmseqs2 has
         the same exposure. What is kept is the part that does not misfire: a non-zero
         exit status, plus the out-of-memory patterns, which matter because a worker can
         be OOM-killed without that reaching the exit status. -->
    <xml name="stdio">
        <stdio>
            <exit_code range=":-1" level="fatal"/>
            <exit_code range="1:" level="fatal"/>
            <regex match="MemoryError" source="both" level="fatal_oom"/>
            <regex match="std::bad_alloc" source="both" level="fatal_oom"/>
            <regex match="java.lang.OutOfMemoryError" source="both" level="fatal_oom"/>
            <regex match="Out of memory" source="both" level="fatal_oom"/>
        </stdio>
    </xml>
</macros>